# 📅 LESSON 8 — THE TWO-DAY RULE

The book also includes the **Two-Day Rule**. ([IIITD Library][2])

The principle:

### Don't allow yourself to abandon a good habit for two consecutive days.

Missed your workout?

Fine.

Missed studying?

It happens.

But don't let:

**1 missed day → 2 → 3 → 10**

become the new habit.

Think:

> **Never miss twice.**

This is particularly powerful for reading.

If you don't read today:

### Read tomorrow.

Even if it's only 5 pages.

---

# 🎯 LESSON 9 — 80/20 RULE

The book includes the **80/20 Rule**, also known as the Pareto Principle. ([IIITD Library][2])

The general idea:

> A relatively small portion of your inputs often produces a large portion of your results.

For studying:

Instead of asking:

> "How can I study everything?"

ask:

> **"Which topics produce the most marks/value?"**

For coding:

Instead of learning 50 technologies:

Focus on the core technologies that produce most of your results.

For career:

Instead of meeting hundreds of random people:

Build strong relationships with a smaller number of valuable connections.

---

# ⏳ LESSON 10 — PARKINSON'S LAW

Another technique explicitly included in the book is **Parkinson's Law**. ([IIITD Library][2])

The principle is:

### **Work tends to expand to fill the time available for its completion.**

Example:

You have:

**7 days to complete an assignment.**

Your brain says:

> "I have plenty of time."

Day 1 — nothing.

Day 2 — nothing.

Day 3 — research.

Day 4 — planning.

Day 5 — more planning.

Day 6 — panic.

Day 7 — complete it.

Now imagine you had **2 days**.

You would probably work with much greater urgency.

So create artificial deadlines.

Instead of:

> "I'll finish this week."

Say:

> **"I'll finish by 7 PM tomorrow."**

